# Low Voltage and the Electrical Alternans Syndrome

- **Low-voltage:**
  - <u>Definition</u>:
    - Peak-to-trough QRS amplitudes of \<= 5mm in all 6 limb leads
    - Peak-to-trough QRS amplitudes of \<= 10mm in all precordial leads
  - <u>DDx</u> - multiple factors may be responsible:
    - Pericardial effusions
    - Pleural effusions
    - Chronic obstructive pulmonary disease
    - Cardiac amyloid
    - Restrictive cardiomyopathy
    - Anasarca
- **Electrical alternans:**
  - **Defiition** - beat-to-beat alternation in one or more component of the ECG signal
  - **DDx:**
    - <u>Pericardial effusion</u> (w/ cardiac tamponade) - combination of sinus tachycardia and electrical alternans is specific for pericardial effusion 
    ![](../img/electrical_alternans_in_cardiac_tamponade.png)
    - <u>Repolarisation abnormalities</u> - variability in ST-T-U complexes suggestive of impending VAs
